  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nHigh pressure aloft will continue to build over the area through the \nweekend, continuing the hot daytime temperatures and elevated \novernight lows. Daily highs will vary little through Saturday \nwith a slight cooling of inland high temperatures Sunday and \nMonday. \n\nElevated overnight lows will offer little to no reprieve from the \ndaytime heat. Coastal areas and inland valleys will see overnight \nlows predominately in the 70s, low deserts in the upper 80s to low \n90s, and the high deserts in the mid 70s to low 80s.\n\nThese high afternoon temperatures and elevated lows will bring \nwidespread moderate HeatRisk to most of the area with major HeatRisk \nin the Inland Empire, San Diego mountains and the low and high \ndeserts through Monday. Major to extreme HeatRisk indicates that \nanyone may be negatively impacted by the heat if the proper \nprecautions are not taken. Make sure to stay hydrated, stay cool, \navoid heavy activity and direct sunlight and check on the elderly, \nchildren and pets.\n\nCoastal low clouds will continue to form in the late evenings into \nthe early morning hours, pushing inland as far as 20 miles in some \nareas through the weekend at times. A slight increase in monsoonal \nmoisture will create mid-level clouds over the mountains today and \nFriday with a 10-12% chance for light rain in the mountains for San \nBernardino and Riverside Counties.\n\nA little uncertainty exists in regards to temperature and moisture \nover the area Sunday. Models differ slightly over the placement of \nthe upper level high pressure. This will affect how much the \nafternoon highs will cool Sunday and the chances for rain and storms \nover the area (currently forecasting 5-15%).\n\nOtherwise, afternoon highs and overnight lows are expected to cool \nslightly by Monday with conditions drying into mid week. \nTemperatures will warm again by mid to late next week as high \npressure aloft once again builds over the area.",
